As discussed, the primary concern is your rights regarding receiving unpaid rent by Tenant. You began renting the property to the Tenant in or about 2022. Throughout her tenancy, Tenant has been consistently late with her rental payments. In or about April 2025, Tenant paid $1,300.00 of the $1,700.00 rent. May 2025, Tenant paid $500.00 of the rent and has failed to pay June 2025 rent. Tenant agreed to vacate the premises by mid June 2025 after you served her with a 3-day notice to pay or vacate the property, but Tenant did not vacate at that time. You are seeking to retain the security deposit and recoup the remainder of rent due under the lease agreement.

Here are the key points of our analysis:

• Florida law establishes that the remedy for tenant being non-compliant with the lease is serving the tenant with a 3-day notice for failure to pay. If the rent is not paid in the time specified in the notice, the landlord has the right to proceed with an eviction action.
• Per the lease agreement, an early termination fee or liquidated damages of two months rent would be charged to the tenant if the lease is terminated early. In this matter, if the Tenant vacated prior to June 30, 2025, you are entitled to charge a liquidated damage of two months rent. Even if the Tenant vacated as a result of the 3-day notice, the law considers this an early termination.
• Pursuant to Florida law, a landlord has 15 days after a tenant vacates to return the security deposit or 30 days to send a notice to impose claim on deposit, via certified mail, to the Tenant’s last known address.
• The notice to impose claim on deposit, must include all reasons and amounts for imposing the claim..
• If the landlord fails to provide the notice within the 30 day period, the landlord waives the right to impose a claim.
• The law also provides that the landlord has the right to file a lawsuit for any alleged damages to the property.

Based on the above, a negotiated resolution would likely be your next most efficient and cost-effective path forward. One option would be to issue a notice to impose claim on security deposit, itemizing all damages and incurred costs. In order to proceed we will need an itemized list of the damages to the property and the account statement for all past due rent. If accepted, this may lead to a joint resolution that includes mutual waivers and a clear release of future liability.

